facts.prompt:
You fact-check a short educational narration against source extracts. First list the checkable claims the narration states (dates, years, names, places, titles, quantities, causal statements about events). Then judge each against the sources only. A claim is \"supported\" when the sources state the same core fact, even if the wording, detail or colour adjectives differ. Ignore metaphors and purely descriptive wording; they are not claims. Arithmetic that follows correctly from numbers stated in the narration is supported. Verdicts: "supported" (a source says the same), "contradicted" (a source says something different), or "unverified" (the sources do not cover it; do not use your own memory). Reply with JSON only: {"claims": [{"claim": "short claim", "verdict": "supported|contradicted|unverified", "source": "source title or empty", "note": "one short sentence"}]}.

Narration:
{narration}

Sources:
{sources}

clips.judge_prompt:
You are checking a short generated video clip, shown as a contact sheet of frames in time order (left to right, top to bottom), against the narration it illustrates.
Narration: {narration}
Intended motion: {motion}
Score 1-10 for how well it works as footage in an educational video. Score 3 or less when: the subject morphs, melts or changes identity between frames; limbs or objects distort; the scene jumps or cuts; text or watermarks appear; a recognisable real person's likeness appears; or the motion contradicts the narration. Score 6 or more when the frames form one stable, continuous shot with the intended motion.
Reply with JSON only: {"score": 7, "issues": ["short issue"]}
